---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

const title = 'Journalism vs content writing career India: the honest comparison';
const description =
  'Journalism vs content writing career India compared on real pay, entry path, job security, gig risk, and how AI is reshaping each field differently in 2026.';
const canonical =
  'https://futurecareerschool.com/blog/career-options/journalism-vs-content-writing-career-india';
const publishedDateISO = '2026-07-14';
const publishedDateLabel = '14 July 2026';
const authorName = 'Allu Vagdevi';
const authorUrl = 'https://futurecareerschool.com/about/allu-vagdevi/';
const authorRole = 'Career researcher and writer';

const jumpLinks = [
  { id: 'short-answer', label: 'The short answer' },
  { id: 'what-the-work-is', label: 'What the daily work actually looks like' },
  { id: 'entry-path', label: 'Entry path: degree, exams, and portfolio' },
  { id: 'employers', label: 'Who actually hires for each' },
  { id: 'salary-reality', label: 'Salary reality: fresher to 5 years' },
  { id: 'job-security', label: 'Job security vs gig risk' },
  { id: 'skill-overlap', label: 'Skill overlap and where they split' },
  { id: 'ai-impact', label: 'How AI is changing both fields' },
  { id: 'checkpoint', label: 'The 4-Checkpoint Protocol for this decision' },
  { id: 'who-fits-journalism', label: 'Who genuinely fits journalism' },
  { id: 'who-fits-content', label: 'Who genuinely fits content writing' },
  { id: 'hybrid-paths', label: 'The hybrid paths nobody mentions' },
  { id: 'gates', label: 'The 3 Gates before you commit' },
  { id: 'switching-later', label: 'Can you switch between them later?' },
  { id: 'mistakes', label: 'Mistakes to avoid' },
  { id: 'next-step', label: 'What to do next' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'FAQs' },
];

const workRows = [
  {
    aspect: 'Core daily task',
    journalism: 'Chase a story, verify facts with at least two sources, interview people, file a report against a hard deadline that someone else sets.',
    content: 'Research a topic, write to a brief a marketing team gave you, target a keyword, and hand in a draft against a deadline you usually had a say in.',
  },
  {
    aspect: 'Where the day gets hard',
    journalism: 'A source cancels an hour before deadline, a fact will not confirm, or a story competes with five other outlets covering the same event.',
    content: 'A client changes the brief midway, an AI-generated first draft needs a full rewrite, or a piece needs to rank on Google and read well for humans at the same time.',
  },
  {
    aspect: 'Who you answer to',
    journalism: 'An editor, a news desk, and ultimately the public record — accuracy and attribution carry legal and reputational weight.',
    content: 'A client, a marketing manager, or an editorial calendar — the piece needs to serve a business goal, not just inform.',
  },
  {
    aspect: 'Main output',
    journalism: 'News reports, investigations, features, explainers, or on-camera segments published under your byline.',
    content: 'Blog posts, website copy, product descriptions, email sequences, social captions, or long-form guides — usually unbylined or lightly credited.',
  },
  {
    aspect: 'Where the ceiling comes from',
    journalism: 'Investigative depth, a beat you own, a following that trusts your byline, and eventually editorial leadership.',
    content: 'Niche subject expertise (SaaS, fintech, health), SEO and content-strategy skill, and the ability to move from writer to content lead or strategist.',
  },
];

const entryRows = [
  {
    route: 'Formal journalism degree/diploma',
    journalism: 'A Bachelor\'s or PG Diploma in Journalism/Mass Communication is the standard route. The Indian Institute of Mass Communication (IIMC) accepts graduates in any discipline with at least 55% marks, admits mainly through CUET-PG, and charges roughly Rs 1.3-2 lakh for its flagship PG Diploma. State universities and private mass-comm colleges offer cheaper, less selective versions of the same degree.',
    content: 'No formal degree is required in most job listings. A degree in English, mass communication, marketing, or any other field is treated as a plus, not a gate.',
  },
  {
    route: 'Portfolio or clips required to get hired',
    journalism: 'Editors expect published clips, internship bylines, or campus-publication work before they will assign a real story. A degree alone rarely gets you past the door at a serious newsroom.',
    content: 'Editors and clients expect 3-5 sample pieces or a live portfolio (blog, LinkedIn articles, or a personal website) before they trust you with paid work. No published clips required — you can create your own samples.',
  },
  {
    route: 'Time to first paid assignment',
    journalism: 'Usually starts with an internship (often unpaid or a stipend of Rs 5,000-15,000/month) at a newsroom, then a stringer or trainee reporter role.',
    content: 'Can start earning within weeks of building a portfolio, through freelance platforms, cold outreach to small businesses, or agency internships.',
  },
  {
    route: 'Gatekeeping and competition',
    journalism: 'Limited seats at reputed institutes, a small number of national-outlet openings each year, and strong competition for bylines at trusted publications.',
    content: 'Low barrier to entry means far more people competing for basic gigs at the bottom, but far less competition for anyone who builds real subject-matter depth.',
  },
];

const employerRows = [
  {
    segment: 'Where journalists get hired',
    detail: 'India has more than 17,000 registered newspapers and roughly 400 news TV channels, but the market is genuinely oversupplied at the regional-channel level, where pay is often Rs 15,000-30,000 a month. National print and digital-first outlets (The Hindu, Hindustan Times, Mint, The Wire, Scroll, The Print, The Quint, Newslaundry) offer stronger pay and more editorial independence, but far fewer openings relative to applicants.',
  },
  {
    segment: 'Where content writers get hired',
    detail: 'Marketing agencies, SaaS and product companies (Zoho, Freshworks, Razorpay-type employers), e-commerce brands, ed-tech firms, and in-house marketing teams across nearly every industry. Content writing is a horizontal skill needed by almost any company with a website, so the employer pool is wider and more spread out than journalism\'s.',
  },
  {
    segment: 'Freelance and gig platforms',
    detail: 'Content writing has a mature freelance market on Upwork, Fiverr, and direct client outreach, with over 15 million Indians earning some income through freelance work as of 2026. Journalism has a much smaller freelance/stringer market, mostly limited to contributing bylines and per-story rates at digital outlets.',
  },
];

const salaryRows = [
  {
    stage: 'Fresher, 0-1 years',
    journalism: 'Rs 1.5-3 LPA at regional outlets and smaller newsrooms; Rs 3-8 LPA for entry-level reporters at national print, TV, or digital-first outlets with a strong portfolio.',
    content: 'Rs 2.5-4.5 LPA at agencies and startups, often paid as Rs 15,000-22,000 a month; niche B2B or SaaS content roles can start higher for candidates with a strong writing sample.',
  },
  {
    stage: '2-4 years experience',
    journalism: 'Roughly Rs 4-9 LPA at established outlets for a reporter who owns a beat, with senior sub-editors and desk heads earning more depending on the publication\'s size and reach.',
    content: 'Rs 4-7 LPA on average; writers who specialise in a paying niche such as B2B SaaS, fintech, or technical documentation can reach Rs 6-18 LPA, well ahead of generalist writers at the same experience level.',
  },
  {
    stage: '5+ years, senior',
    journalism: 'Senior correspondent, bureau chief, or editor roles at national outlets pay meaningfully more, but openings are limited and depend heavily on the outlet\'s size, reach, and financial health.',
    content: 'Senior content strategists and heads of content at product companies typically earn Rs 10-20 LPA, with niche specialists and AI-augmented senior writers commanding a further 20-35% premium over generalists.',
  },
  {
    stage: 'Typical pay pattern',
    journalism: 'Salary-led. Most journalists are on a fixed monthly salary at one employer, with per-story freelance income as a smaller add-on for some.',
    content: 'Mixed. Many content writers combine a salaried role with freelance projects, or work entirely on a freelance/retainer basis once they have a client base.',
  },
];

const jobSecurityGrid = [
  {
    label: 'Journalism',
    title: 'Fewer, more structured jobs — but the pool is shrinking',
    body: 'A journalism job usually comes with a fixed salary, a defined role, and (at established outlets) some legal protection under India\'s Working Journalists Act. But the sector is not stable right now: Indian media layoffs rose roughly 15% year-on-year through 2025, and global media and entertainment companies cut more than 17,000 jobs in 2025 alone, with the pace continuing through 2026 as print advertising keeps declining and outlets consolidate.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Content writing',
    title: 'More total opportunities — but most of them are gig-shaped',
    body: 'Content writing has more entry points (agency jobs, in-house roles, freelance gigs) but a much larger share of the work is inherently gig-based: per-piece freelance rates, retainer contracts that can end anytime, and heavy competition for basic writing tasks now that generic AI drafting exists. Income is real but far less predictable month to month unless you build a retainer client base or land a stable in-house role.',
  },
];

const skillOverlapItems = [
  {
    label: 'Shared skill',
    title: 'Research and structure',
    body: 'Both careers demand the ability to take a messy topic, find what actually matters, and organise it into something a reader can follow quickly. Neither field forgives sloppy structure or a buried lead.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Shared skill',
    title: 'Clear, disciplined writing',
    body: 'Short sentences, active voice, and cutting anything that does not serve the reader. A journalist and a content writer are graded on the same core writing craft, even though the end product looks different.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Journalism-specific',
    title: 'Verification, sourcing, and media law awareness',
    body: 'Fact-checking with multiple sources, understanding defamation risk, protecting a source, and reporting under pressure without getting the facts wrong. There is no real content-writing equivalent to this discipline.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Content-writing-specific',
    title: 'SEO, conversion, and brand voice',
    body: 'Writing for a keyword and a search intent, matching a brand\'s tone across dozens of pieces, and writing lines that are meant to move someone toward an action, not just inform them. Journalism rarely trains this muscle directly.',
  },
];

const checkpointItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Fit',
    body: 'Journalism rewards people who get energy from chasing a moving story, talking to strangers under pressure, and publishing on someone else\'s clock. Content writing rewards people who prefer a steadier, more planned writing process — a brief, a deadline you can usually negotiate, and fewer live, unpredictable moments.',
    note: 'If a source going silent an hour before deadline sounds thrilling rather than stressful, that is a real signal toward journalism.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Context',
    body: 'Can your family runway absorb an unpaid or low-stipend journalism internship (often Rs 5,000-15,000/month) for a year or more before a stable reporting role appears? Content writing usually lets you start earning something within weeks through freelance work, even if the early income is inconsistent.',
    note: 'Journalism\'s entry period is often longer and lower-paid than content writing\'s, even though journalism\'s ceiling can carry more prestige.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Market',
    body: 'Journalism has a shrinking number of stable staff roles concentrated at fewer trusted outlets. Content writing has a much wider, more horizontal employer base, but a large share of that market is gig work with no guaranteed monthly income.',
    note: 'More total opportunities (content writing) is not the same as more job security — read the gig-risk section below before assuming content writing is the "safer" pick.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Survival',
    body: 'AI is pressuring both fields at their easiest layer. In content writing, that means routine, templated copy that a generic AI tool can now draft in seconds. In journalism, that means basic wire-style rewrites and routine summaries. In both, the safer position is the person who verifies, adds a genuine human angle, or owns a relationship an AI tool cannot replicate.',
    note: 'The real question is not "will AI take this job." It is "which part of this specific role still needs a human to own the judgment call."',
  },
];

const journalismFitItems = [
  {
    label: 'Genuine fit',
    title: 'You want your name on the work',
    body: 'A byline matters to you, and you want the public record, not just a client, to know you wrote something. Content writing rarely offers that kind of visible, individual credit.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Genuine fit',
    title: 'You can hold up under real-time pressure',
    body: 'Deadlines in journalism are often set by events, not by you. If a breaking story energises you instead of panicking you, that instinct matters more than most journalism-school coursework.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Genuine fit',
    title: 'You care about accuracy more than persuasion',
    body: 'You would rather report an uncomfortable, well-sourced fact than write a sentence designed to make a brand look good. That instinct is the core of editorial integrity, and it is hard to fake in an interview.',
  },
];

const contentFitItems = [
  {
    label: 'Genuine fit',
    title: 'You want more control over your schedule and income streams',
    body: 'You would rather manage two or three clients or projects on your own terms than answer to one newsroom\'s daily deadline. Content writing rewards people who can self-manage without an editor checking in constantly.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Genuine fit',
    title: 'You like adapting your voice to different goals',
    body: 'Writing a playful Instagram caption on Monday and a technical SaaS explainer on Tuesday does not feel like a compromise to you — it feels like a useful, flexible skill.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Genuine fit',
    title: 'You are comfortable treating writing as a business skill',
    body: 'Pricing your work, pitching clients, negotiating scope, and building a portfolio like a small business owner would. Content writing rewards this instinct far more directly than journalism does.',
  },
];

const hybridItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Branded content / native journalist',
    body: 'Writes sponsored or branded content for a news outlet\'s commercial team — uses journalistic research and clarity skills but writes to serve a brand\'s message, sitting deliberately between both worlds.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Corporate communications and PR writer',
    body: 'Uses journalism-style news judgment and interviewing skill to write press releases, executive statements, and thought-leadership pieces for a company, often hiring former journalists specifically for their credibility and instincts.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Freelance explainer and newsletter writer',
    body: 'Builds an independent newsletter or explainer publication using journalism-grade research discipline, monetised the way a content business is monetised — subscriptions, sponsorships, or a paid membership tier.',
  },
];

const mistakeItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Choosing journalism only for the prestige of a byline, without checking the pay reality',
    body: 'A visible byline at a well-known outlet does not always come with strong pay, especially at regional channels where salaries can sit at Rs 15,000-30,000 a month. Check actual entry-level pay at the specific outlets you are targeting, not the outlet\'s brand reputation.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Choosing content writing only because "anyone can start," without building a real niche',
    body: 'Generic content writing is the layer AI tools have commoditised fastest. Writers who stay generalists compete on price against AI-assisted output; writers who build depth in one paying niche (SaaS, fintech, health, technical documentation) keep real pricing power.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Assuming a mass communication degree alone guarantees a reporting job',
    body: 'Editors hire on published clips and demonstrated news judgment far more than on the college name on your degree. Build real clips during your degree through internships and campus publications, not after you graduate.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Treating content writing income as guaranteed because "freelancing is flexible"',
    body: 'Flexible does not mean stable. Freelance content income swings widely — from roughly Rs 25,000 to Rs 2 lakh a month — depending on niche, client relationships, and how consistently you pitch. Build a retainer base before treating freelance content writing as your only income.',
  },
  {
    num: '05',
    title: 'Ignoring how differently AI is hitting each field',
    body: 'AI threatens routine, templated tasks in both fields, but the specific pressure is different — trust and verification in journalism, commoditised generic drafting in content writing. Pick the field that matches your fit, then build the specific AI-resistant skill that field actually needs.',
  },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'Journalism vs content writing career India: which pays more?',
    a: 'At the entry level, they are close: journalism fresher pay ranges from Rs 1.5-3 LPA at regional outlets to Rs 3-8 LPA at strong national outlets, while content writing freshers typically earn Rs 2.5-4.5 LPA at agencies and startups. Past 2-4 years, content writers who build a paying niche (B2B SaaS, fintech, technical documentation) often out-earn general-beat journalists, reaching Rs 6-18 LPA, while senior journalism pay depends heavily on the specific outlet\'s size and financial health.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which is more stable, journalism or content writing, in India right now?',
    a: 'Neither is fully stable, but the risk looks different. Journalism offers more structured, salaried roles but a shrinking pool of them — Indian media layoffs rose roughly 15% year-on-year through 2025 amid print-revenue decline and industry consolidation. Content writing offers a wider employer base but a much larger share of gig and freelance work, where income depends on client relationships rather than a fixed salary.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need a journalism degree to become a journalist in India?',
    a: 'It helps but is not always mandatory. A Bachelor\'s or PG Diploma in Journalism/Mass Communication from an institute like IIMC is the standard route and the most direct way to build contacts and internships, but many working journalists in India hold degrees in English, political science, or other fields and built their case through internships and published clips instead.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can a content writer become a journalist later, or the reverse?',
    a: 'Yes, and it happens in both directions. Content writers moving into journalism usually need to build actual reporting clips — pitch and publish a few genuine reported pieces at a digital outlet — since editors hire on demonstrated news judgment, not general writing samples. Journalists moving into content writing usually need to build SEO and brand-voice skills and adjust to writing for a business goal instead of pure public interest.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is content writing a good backup if I do not get into journalism?',
    a: 'It can be, but treat it as its own decision, not a consolation prize. Content writing rewards a different set of instincts — client management, SEO, brand adaptability — and people who enter it reluctantly, without building real skill in those areas, tend to stay stuck as generic, lower-paid writers competing against AI-assisted output.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is AI going to replace journalists or content writers first?',
    a: 'Neither is being replaced wholesale, but the easy, templated layer is shrinking in both. In content writing, generic AI-drafted copy is already commoditised, and generalist writers face the most price pressure. In journalism, AI is mostly used for support tasks such as converting text into short video or summarising routine updates, while original reporting, verification, and source relationships stay firmly human — Indian news consumers themselves report growing concern about fake news and misinformation, which raises the value of verified, trusted reporting rather than lowering it.',
  },
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a },
  })),
};
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-options"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Journalism vs content writing career India comes down to one honest trade: journalism gives you a
    visible byline, structured legal protections, and public-interest work, but a shrinking pool of stable
    roles; content writing gives you a much wider, more flexible employer base and faster income, but a
    market that leans heavily on gig work and is getting squeezed hardest by generic AI drafting.</strong>
    Neither path is the "safe" one right now, and both are being reshaped by AI at very different layers of the
    job. The people who actually win in either field are the ones who build a genuine, provable
    <strong>skill portfolio</strong> instead of picking based on prestige or on which one sounds easier to
    start. That decision, made with real numbers instead of guesswork, is what moves you toward stronger
    income and earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Fresher pay is close: Rs 1.5-8 LPA for journalism depending on the outlet, Rs 2.5-4.5 LPA for content writing at agencies and startups.</li>
      <li>Journalism jobs are more structured but shrinking — Indian media layoffs rose roughly 15% year-on-year through 2025, alongside 17,000+ global media job cuts in 2025 alone.</li>
      <li>Content writing has a wider employer base but a much larger gig/freelance share, with monthly freelance income swinging from roughly Rs 25,000 to Rs 2 lakh depending on niche and client base.</li>
      <li>AI hits each field differently: it commoditises generic content writing fastest, while in journalism it mostly supports routine tasks and raises the value of verified, trusted reporting.</li>
      <li><strong>Content writers who build a paying niche (SaaS, fintech, technical writing) often out-earn general-beat journalists past the 2-4 year mark, but generalist content writers face the steepest AI-driven price pressure of anyone in either field.</strong></li>
      <li>Test your actual fit with one real reported piece and one real client-brief piece before committing years to either lane.</li>
      <li>Long-term leverage comes from subject expertise, verification, AI-assisted research, clear storytelling, relationships, audience trust, and a visible body of work. Those assets can travel into specialist media, consulting, newsletters, research, or owned content.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    If you are still weighing this against other career-options paths after 12th or graduation, browse more
    <a href="/blog/career-options/">career options guides</a> before narrowing down to writing-based work
    specifically.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you want a second opinion on which of these actually fits how you think and work, a session with
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">career guidance</a>
    can help you weigh the trade-offs against your specific situation, family runway, and timeline.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer to journalism vs content writing career India</h2>

  <p>
    There is no universal winner here, and most comparison articles pick a side too fast because "writer"
    sounds like one job when it is really two very different careers wearing the same word.
  </p>

  <p>
    Journalism wins on prestige, public-interest impact, and structured legal protection for staff roles, but
    the pool of stable positions is genuinely shrinking as print revenue declines and outlets consolidate.
  </p>

  <p>
    Content writing wins on flexibility, faster entry, and a much wider employer base, but a large share of
    that market is gig-shaped, and the generic end of it is exactly where AI tools are applying the most price
    pressure right now.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Both fields get romanticised for the wrong reasons. Journalism gets sold as "changing the world through
      the truth," which undersells the years of low pay and instability most reporters actually face before
      any real stability. Content writing gets sold as "easy money working from anywhere," which undersells
      how brutal the price competition has become for anyone who stays a generalist. The honest answer sits
      in the specific niche and specific skill depth you build, not the field label on your LinkedIn headline.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="what-the-work-is">What the daily work actually looks like in each career</h2>

  <p>
    Most people compare journalism and content writing using the word "writer" as if the daily reality were
    the same. It is not.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Aspect of the work</th>
          <th>Journalism</th>
          <th>Content writing</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {workRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Aspect of the work"><strong>{item.aspect}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Journalism">{item.journalism}</td>
            <td data-label="Content writing">{item.content}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Notice the real split is not "who writes better sentences." It is who you are ultimately accountable to —
    the public record and an editor in journalism, versus a client's business goal in content writing — and
    that single difference shapes almost everything else about each career.
  </p>

  <h2 id="entry-path">Entry path: degree, exams, and portfolio</h2>

  <p>
    The two fields also differ sharply in how you actually get your first paid assignment.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Entry factor</th>
          <th>Journalism</th>
          <th>Content writing</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {entryRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Entry factor"><strong>{item.route}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Journalism">{item.journalism}</td>
            <td data-label="Content writing">{item.content}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      A journalism degree from a strong institute genuinely opens doors that pure hustle cannot always match
      in this specific field, because editors trust structured training and campus-publication clips more than
      a cold pitch from a stranger. Content writing runs the opposite way — a strong portfolio built on your
      own time beats a marketing degree almost every time an editor or client is actually deciding who to hire.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="employers">Who actually hires for each career</h2>

  <p>
    Where you can realistically get hired matters as much as the job title itself.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Employer segment</th>
          <th>What it actually looks like</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {employerRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Employer segment"><strong>{item.segment}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What it actually looks like">{item.detail}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Journalism employer shape</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Narrower, more concentrated pool: national outlets, regional channels, and digital-native newsrooms.</li>
        <li>Regional-channel pay often sits at Rs 15,000-30,000 a month, well below national-outlet pay.</li>
        <li>Editorial independence and byline value are highest at trusted national and digital-native outlets.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Content writing employer shape</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Wide, horizontal pool: agencies, SaaS and product companies, e-commerce, and in-house marketing teams.</li>
        <li>Freelance and gig platforms add a genuinely large parallel income route, used by over 15 million Indians.</li>
        <li>Highest-paying niches (B2B SaaS, fintech) sit inside product companies, not generic content mills.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="salary-reality">Salary reality: fresher to 5 years, without the highlight-reel numbers</h2>

  <p>
    Salary comparison content for this exact decision tends to quote the best-case number for whichever field
    it is promoting. Here is the range you can actually expect at each stage.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Career stage</th>
          <th>Journalism</th>
          <th>Content writing</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {salaryRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Career stage"><strong>{item.stage}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Journalism">{item.journalism}</td>
            <td data-label="Content writing">{item.content}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Ranges are directional, drawn from current salary-tracking sources, hiring reports, and industry coverage
    at the time of writing. Verify current figures against live postings and specific employers before making
    a financial decision.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      "Content writing pays more" is not universally true — it is true specifically for writers who build a
      paying niche. A generalist content writer competing purely on speed and volume is now competing against
      AI-assisted drafting, and that comparison does not favour the human. A journalist who owns a real beat at
      a trusted outlet still has a genuine, AI-resistant value the market pays for.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="job-security">Job security vs gig risk</h2>

  <p>
    This is the part most "which career is better" content skips, and it matters more than the salary numbers
    above.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    {jobSecurityGrid.map((item) => (
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <div class="feature-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    Neither number should be read as "journalism is doomed" or "content writing is a scam." Both statements
    are true at once: journalism has fewer roles that are individually more structured, and content writing
    has more roles that are individually less predictable. Pick based on which risk shape you can actually
    live with, not which one sounds scarier in a headline.
  </p>

  <h2 id="skill-overlap">Skill overlap and where the two fields split</h2>

  <p>
    The two careers share more foundation than people assume, then diverge sharply on a few specific skills.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    {skillOverlapItems.map((item) => (
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <div class="feature-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    That overlap is exactly why people move between the two fields more often than career-guide diagrams
    suggest — the core writing craft transfers, and only the specialised layer (verification and sourcing on
    one side, SEO and brand voice on the other) needs to be built fresh.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai-impact">How AI is changing both fields, in different ways</h2>

  <p>
    A common mistake in this comparison is assuming AI threatens only content writing ("AI can write blog
    posts now") while journalism stays untouched. That is incomplete.
  </p>

  <p>
    In content writing, generic AI-drafted copy is already commoditised, and over 42% of content writers now
    use AI tools daily or a few times a week just to keep pace, which pushes down what a purely generalist
    writer can charge. In journalism, AI shows up more as a production support tool — outlets like Scroll.in
    use it to convert text articles into short video for social platforms, and some legacy outlets have
    experimented with AI-generated news segments — while original reporting and source verification stay
    firmly human.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      In both fields, AI is shrinking the "easy, templated" layer and growing demand for the person who
      verifies, adds a genuine human angle, or owns a client or source relationship an AI tool cannot
      replicate. Indian news consumers themselves report rising concern about fake news and misinformation,
      which raises the value of trusted, verified reporting rather than lowering it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Rather than asking "which field is safer from AI," a sharper question is: in whichever lane you pick, are
    you building the judgment, verification, or relationship layer AI still cannot do, or are you doing the
    routine task AI is about to do faster than you?
  </p>

  <h2 id="checkpoint">Use The 4-Checkpoint Protocol before you commit to either path</h2>

  <p>
    A salary table cannot tell you which one fits your actual temperament and life situation. The
    4-Checkpoint Protocol narrows this decision to what genuinely matters for you.
    <strong>Getting this fit right early is what actually shapes your income trajectory and how soon
    you reach financial freedom — not which field sounds more prestigious on paper.</strong>
