  <p>
    If neither pure lane feels like a perfect fit after the checkpoints above, one of these hybrid roles is
    often the more realistic first target than forcing yourself into either extreme.
  </p>

  <h2 id="gates">Pass The 3 Gates before you spend years on this</h2>

  <p>
    The 4-Checkpoint Protocol tells you which lane fits on paper. The 3 Gates make you test it in the real
    world before you commit a degree or years of income to it.
  </p>

  <div class="gates-block">
    <p class="gates-block__title">
      Do not lock in a journalism degree or quit a job for full-time freelance content writing before passing
      all three gates in your chosen lane.
    </p>
    <div class="gates-row">
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 1</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of skill</span>
        <p>
          For journalism, report and file one genuine, verified story on a real local or campus issue, not a
          rehashed press release. For content writing, write one real piece for an actual small business or
          your own niche blog, not a generic "5 tips" filler post.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 2</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of communication</span>
        <p>
          For journalism, pitch your story idea to an editor in two sentences and see if it lands. For content
          writing, explain in under two minutes why your piece serves the client's actual business goal, not
          just why it reads well.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 3</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of value</span>
        <p>
          Show the work to a working journalist or a working content professional and ask one direct
          question: "Would this get published or paid for at your outlet or company?" Use their answer, not
          your own hope, to decide.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="switching-later">Can you switch between them later?</h2>

  <p>
    Yes, and the switch happens in both directions more often than most career guides admit.
  </p>

  <p>
    Content writers moving into journalism usually need to build genuine reporting clips — pitching and
    publishing a few real, verified stories at a digital outlet — since editors hire on demonstrated news
    judgment, not general writing samples. Their writing craft is a real head start; what they are missing is
    sourcing discipline and news instinct, not sentence-level skill.
  </p>

  <p>
    Journalists moving into content writing usually need to build SEO fluency and a business-first mindset,
    since journalism trains you to serve the public interest, not a brand's conversion goal. Their research
    and verification instincts transfer well and often make them stronger, more trustworthy content writers
    than people who never had that discipline.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      Neither switch is instant, and neither switch is rare. Treat your first choice as a strong starting
      lane, not a life sentence — the skill overlap between journalism and content writing is bigger than
      most comparison charts suggest.
    </p>
  </div>

